  "markdown": "|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| International Standard Book Number \u00a0\u2014\u00a0 Click for https://mathworld.wolfram.com/ISBN.html | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| Search JOS Website | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| JOS Home Page | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| JOS Online Publications | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| Index of terms in JOS Website | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| Index: Physical Audio Signal Processing | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| Physical Audio Signal Processing | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| Acoustic Modeling with Delay | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| Acoustic Modeling with Delay | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| A Software Delay Line | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| Noise (in signal processing) is a random signal. In the language of statistical signal processing, a noise signal is typically modeled as 'stochastic process', which is in turn defined as a sequence of random variables. \u00a0\u2014\u00a0 Click for https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/sasp/What\\_Noise.html | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| According to Hooke's law, the force F exerted by a spring stretched a distance x from its rest position is F=-kx. \u00a0\u2014\u00a0 Click for http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spring\\_%28device%29 | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| A signal is typically a real-valued function of time. A discrete-time signal is typically a real-valued function of discrete time, and is therefore a time-ordered sequence of real numbers. \u00a0\u2014\u00a0 Click for http://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/filters/Definition\\_Signal.html | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| Click for https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/wg.html | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| A _delay effect_ is an audio effect implemented using a delay line. Nowadays, _digital_ delay lines are most often used. Examples of digital audio delay effects include flanging, the Leslie effect, and artificial reverberation. Often the delay line simulates time delay associated with physical sound propagation. \u00a0\u2014\u00a0 Click for https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/Acoustic\\_Modeling\\_Delay.html | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| Traveling waves propagate (i.e. travel) through space. \u00a0\u2014\u00a0 Click for http://ccrma.stanford.edu/realsimple/travelingwaves | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| Click for http://ccrma.stanford.edu/realsimple/Delay/Delay\\_lines.html | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| Search JOS Website | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| JOS Home Page | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| JOS Online Publications | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| Index of terms in JOS Website | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| Index: Physical Audio Signal Processing | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| Physical Audio Signal Processing | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| Acoustic Modeling with Delay | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| Acoustic Modeling with Delay | |\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| |     |\n| --- |\n| A Software Delay Line | |\n\n **[Next](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/Software_Delay_Line.html) \u00a0\\|**\n**[Prev](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/Acoustic_Modeling_Digital_Delay.html) \u00a0\\|**\n**[Up](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/Acoustic_Modeling_Digital_Delay.html) \u00a0\\|**\n**[Top](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/%7Ejos/pasp/pasp.html)**\n**\\|**\n**[Index](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/Index_this_Document.html) \u00a0\\|**\n**[JOS Index](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/GlobalJOSIndex.html) \u00a0\\|**\n**[JOS Pubs](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pubs.html) \u00a0\\|**\n**[JOS Home](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/%7Ejos) \u00a0\\|**\n**[Search](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/index.html#searchform)**\n\n* * *\n\n## [Delay Lines](http://ccrma.stanford.edu/realsimple/Delay/Delay_lines.html)\n\nThe _delay line_ is an elementary functional\nunit which models _acoustic [propagation](http://ccrma.stanford.edu/realsimple/travelingwaves) delay_. It is a\nfundamental building block of both [delay-effects](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/Acoustic_Modeling_Delay.html) processors and\ndigital- [waveguide synthesis](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/wg.html) models. The function of a delay line is\nto introduce a time delay between its input and output, as shown in\nFig. [2.1](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/Delay_Lines.html#fig:delay).\n\n![\\includegraphics{eps/delay}](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/img36.png)\n\n![% latex2html id marker 79492 $\\textstyle \\parbox{\\twidth }{\\caption{\\protect\\small The $M$-sample delay line.}}$](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/img35.png)\n\nLet the input [signal](http://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/filters/Definition_Signal.html) be denoted x(n),n=0,1,2,\u2026, and let the\ndelay-line length be M samples. Then the output signal y(n) is\nspecified by the relation\n\n(1)y(n)=x(n\u2212M),n=0,1,2,\u2026\n\nwhere x(n)=\u03940 for n<0.\n\nBefore the digital era, delay lines were expensive and imprecise in\n\\`\\`analog'' form. For example, \\`\\` [spring](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spring_%28device%29) reverberators'' (common in\nguitar amplifiers) use metal springs as analog delay lines; while\nadequate for that purpose, they are highly dispersive and prone to\n[noise](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/sasp/What_Noise.html) pick-up. Large delays require prohibitively long springs or\ncoils in analog implementations. In the digital domain, on the other\nhand, delay by N samples is trivially implemented, and non-integer\ndelays can be implemented using interpolation techniques, as discussed\nlater in \u00a7 [4.1](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/Delay_Line_Interpolation.html#sec:delaylineinterp).\n\n* * *\n\n**Subsections**\n\n- [A Software Delay Line](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/Software_Delay_Line.html)\n\n* * *\n\n**[Next](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/Software_Delay_Line.html) \u00a0\\|**\n**[Prev](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/Acoustic_Modeling_Digital_Delay.html) \u00a0\\|**\n**[Up](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/Acoustic_Modeling_Digital_Delay.html) \u00a0\\|**\n**[Top](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/%7Ejos/pasp/pasp.html)**\n**\\|**\n**[Index](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/Index_this_Document.html) \u00a0\\|**\n**[JOS Index](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/GlobalJOSIndex.html) \u00a0\\|**\n**[JOS Pubs](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pubs.html) \u00a0\\|**\n**[JOS Home](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/%7Ejos) \u00a0\\|**\n**[Search](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/index.html#searchform)**\n\n* * *\n\n[\\[How to cite this work\\]](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/pasp-citation.html) [\\[Order a printed hardcopy\\]](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/pasp-hardcopy.html) [\\[Comment on this page via email\\]](mailto:jos@ccrma.stanford.edu?subject=Physical%20Audio%20Signal%20Processing&body=[Please%20paste%20the%20page%20URL%20here%20and%20then%20comment.])\n\n* * *\n\n##### \\`\\` [Physical Audio Signal Processing](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/%7Ejos/pasp/)'',  by [Julius O. Smith III](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/%7Ejos/),  [W3K Publishing](http://www.w3k.org/books/), 2010, [ISBN](https://mathworld.wolfram.com/ISBN.html) 978-0-9745607-2-4   Copyright \u00a9 _2026-03-17_ by [Julius O. Smith III](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/%7Ejos/)  [Center for Computer Research in Music and Acoustics (CCRMA),](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/) [Stanford University](http://www.stanford.edu/)  [![CCRMA](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/%7Ejos/icons/CCRMALogoSmall.gif)](https://ccrma.stanford.edu/)\n\n### SASP Course\n\nProgressExercisesHelp\n\n0 of 306 pages visited\n\n\n**Current Page**\n\nDelay Lines\n\n\n**Exercises Completed**\n\n0 exercises\n\n\nReset Progress\n\nExercises for this page will appear here.\n\nGet AI help understanding this page:\n\nAsk Claude\n\nAsk ChatGPT\n\nAsk Gemini\n\nAsk Perplexity\n\nAsk Grok\n\n\nCopies page context to clipboard, then opens AI in new tab. Just paste!",
